        "output": "```rust\nuse std::collections::HashMap;\nuse std::str::Chars;\n\n/// Checks if brackets in a UTF-8 string are balanced.\n///\n/// Supports (), [], and {}.\n///\n/// Time complexity: O(n), where n is the length of the string.\n/// Space complexity: O(n) in the worst case (stack usage proportional to input length).\npub fn is_balanced(s: &str) -> bool {\n    let mut stack: Vec<char> = vec[];\n\n    // Map closing brackets to their corresponding opening brackets\n    let bracket_map: HashMap<char, char> = [\n        (')', '('),\n        (']', '['),\n        ('}', '{'),\n    ]\n    .iter()\n    .cloned()\n    .collect();\n\n    for c in s.chars() {\n        match c {\n            '(' | '[' | '{' => {\n                stack.push(c);\n            }\n            ')' | ']' | '}' => {\n                if stack.is_empty() {\n                    return false;\n                }\n                let top = stack.pop().unwrap();\n                if top != bracket_map[&c] {\n                    return false;\n                }\n            }\n            _ => {} // Ignore non-bracket characters\n        }\n    }\n\n    stack.is_empty()\n}\n```\n\n### Explanation\n\n- **Algorithm**: We use a stack to keep track of opening brackets. As we iterate through the string:\n  - If we encounter an opening bracket (`(`, `[`, `{`), we push it onto the stack.\n  - If we encounter a closing bracket (`)`, `]`, `}`), we check if the stack is empty (unbalanced) or if the top of the stack matches the corresponding opening bracket. If not, the string is unbalanced.\n- **Time Complexity**: Each character is processed exactly once, so the time complexity is **O(n)**, where `",
